item.content:encoded:
Most landing-zone guides stop after "click Set up landing zone". This one keeps going. By the end, you will have a working multi-account AWS environment where every account has one job, the rules are enforced by Service Control Policies, and the network is owned by one account and shared with the others. Each rule is tested from the command line, so you can prove it holds.Everything here was built in a real organization, including the parts that went wrong: an SCP that broke DNS, an "allow list" SCP that allowed everything, and a RAM share that failed for 40 minutes. The fixes are part of the steps and are collected in the Troubleshooting section.This is part 1 of a series. Part 2 sets up the Build account (CodeCommit, ECR with cross-account pull, and CodeBuild inside the shared VPC), and part 3 runs the application on ECS in Dev and Production.Seven accounts, grouped into Organizational Units (OUs), each with one responsibility: (Root OU) owns AWS Organizations, Control Tower, IAM Identity Center, SCPs. Must not: run workloads. (Security OU) owns security tooling and findings (Config aggregator, GuardDuty, Security Hub). (Security OU) owns central CloudTrail and Config logs. (Infrastructure OU) owns all VPCs, subnets, routes, NAT and internet gateways. Must not: run applications or CI/CD. (Deployments OU) owns source repository, CodeBuild, CodePipeline, ECR. Must not: own networking, run applications. (Workloads OU) owns the application in the dev environment. Must not: own networking, build or push images. (Workloads OU) owns the application in production. Must not: own networking, build or push images.The "must not" rules matter as much as ownership: each one becomes a Deny statement in an SCP, so it holds even for an administrator inside that account.What you will have at the end:An organization with five OUs and seven accounts, governed by Control Tower (section 4).Single sign-on to every account through IAM Identity Center, with no IAM users or access keys anywhere (section 4).Four deny-based SCPs that enforce each account's job (section 5).Three VPCs owned by the Network account, each shared only with its own environment (section 6).A script that proves all of the above in one run (section 7).The diagram already shows the Build pipeline and the workloads. They are built on this foundation in parts 2 and 3.2. Why one account per responsibility?An AWS account is a hard boundary for IAM permissions, billing, service quotas, and the blast radius of mistakes. When dev, prod, networking, and CI/CD share one account, a developer who needs the dev cluster easily ends up with access to production, and costs can only be split with careful tagging. Separate accounts give you these boundaries for free, and OUs let you apply the same rules to a group of accounts at once: every account you add to the Workloads OU later inherits its guardrails.The design follows one principle, ownership: for any resource, it should be obvious which account owns it and who is allowed to change it. The network belongs to the Network account, images belong to the Build account, and the running application belongs to the workload account. Images are built once and only pulled by Dev and Production, so Production runs exactly what was tested in Dev.This article builds only Dev and Production. A Staging account can be added later in exactly the same way.3. Prerequisites and costAn AWS account that is not yet part of an organization. It becomes the management account. Don't use an account that runs workloads.A separate email address for each new account. With Gmail, you can use +aliases such as you+network@gmail.com.AWS CLI v2. All access in this article goes through IAM Identity Center (SSO). We don't create a single IAM user or access key, and the SCPs will even forbid it.About two hours of hands-on time, plus about 35 minutes of waiting while Control Tower sets up.Region: everything runs in eu-central-1 (Frankfurt). Control Tower governs only this Region, and an SCP denies the others. Replace it with your own Region throughout.Control Tower itself is free, but the services it enables are not. Approximate monthly costs in eu-central-1 for this setup:Config, CloudTrail, S3 logsA few dollars at this scaleThe NAT gateways are almost all of the bill. If you build this only to learn, follow the Cleanup section when you are done, or delete the NAT gateways between sessions.Control Tower creates the organization structure, the two shared accounts in the Security OU, an organization-wide CloudTrail trail, and AWS Config in every enrolled account. Everything later in this article builds on it.Before you start: if IAM Identity Center is already enabled in the management account, Control Tower must be set up in the same Region. Choose the home Region carefully. Changing it later means decommissioning the landing zone.Step 1: Set up the landing zoneSign in to the management account and open AWS Control Tower → Set up landing zone.On the first page, choose your home Region and Automatic account enrollment. With automatic enrollment, accounts that join the organization later (and accounts you move between OUs) get the Control Tower baseline and controls without manual enrollment.On the second page, keep the recommended Sandbox OU. It is a place for experiments with fewer restrictions, and we will also use it to test new SCPs before they reach other OUs.Next, Control Tower asks for the two shared accounts in the Security OU:Log Archive: stores the CloudTrail and AWS Config logs of every account. I named it "CloudTrail administrator".Audit: aggregates AWS Config data and becomes the delegated administrator for security services. I named it "Aggregator account".You will see these names in the screenshots. Each account needs its own email address. With Gmail, a +alias such as you+audit@gmail.com is enough: mail to it arrives in your normal inbox.Review the settings and confirm:The setup takes about 35 minutes. You can follow each step on the landing zone page:Control Tower created the Security and Sandbox OUs. Create the other three under Root in Control Tower → Organization → Create resources → Create organizational unit: Infrastructure, Deployments, and Workloads. Create them through Control Tower, not the Organizations console, so that they are registered and get the Control Tower controls.If you expect many workloads, you can nest OUs later, for example Workloads/web and Workloads/ai, and attach different policies to each.Step 3: Create the accountsUse Control Tower → Account Factory → Create account for each workload account:Account Factory provisions accounts one at a time, so creating all four takes a while. When it's done, Control Tower → Organization should show every OU as Registered and every account as Enrolled:Step 4: Access through IAM Identity CenterControl Tower configures IAM Identity Center. It creates groups such as AWSControlTowerAdmins, AWSSecurityAuditors, and AWSLogArchiveViewers, and permission sets such as AWSAdministratorAccess, AWSPowerUserAccess, and AWSReadOnlyAccess. In every enrolled account, your user gets AWSAdministratorAccess directly, while the AWSControlTowerAdmins group only gets AWSOrganizationsFullAccess. That direct assignment is how I sign in everywhere in this article:For a team, don't give everyone administrator access. Create groups by role and assign each group only to the accounts it needs:: AWSAdministratorAccess in all accounts: AWSAdministratorAccess in Network: AWSPowerUserAccess in Dev: AWSReadOnlyAccess in ProductionIn the console: IAM Identity Center → Groups → Create group, then AWS accounts → select the accounts → Assign users or groups → choose the group and the permission set. The same assignment from the CLI:aws sso-admin create-account-assignment --profile mgmt \
  --instance-arn <identity-center-instance-arn> \
  --target-type AWS_ACCOUNT --target-id <dev-account-id> \
  --permission-set-arn <power-user-permission-set-arn> \
  --principal-type GROUP --principal-id <developers-group-id>
For the command line, add one profile per account to ~/.aws/config, then sign in once with aws sso login --sso-session lz. One sign-in covers all profiles:[sso-session lz]
sso_start_url = https://<your-portal>.awsapps.com/start
sso_region = eu-central-1
sso_registration_scopes = sso:account:access

[profile mgmt]
sso_session = lz
sso_account_id = <management-account-id>
sso_role_name = AWSAdministratorAccess
region = eu-central-1

[profile network]
sso_session = lz
sso_account_id = <network-account-id>
sso_role_name = AWSAdministratorAccess
region = eu-central-1

# ...same for audit, logarchive, build, dev, prod
 use the role name that you actually see in the access portal. In my organization the management account has AdministratorAccess while member accounts have AWSAdministratorAccess. A wrong role name fails with "ForbiddenException: No access".Also check that no AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID or AWS_SESSION_TOKEN environment variables are set in your shell. The CLI uses them whenever a command has no --profile, so a single forgotten flag silently runs that command against a completely different account. I had exactly this situation, with keys for an unrelated organization in my shell.5. Service Control PoliciesService Control Policies (SCPs) are guardrails in AWS Organizations. They define the maximum permissions available to IAM users and roles in the accounts of an OU, regardless of what their IAM policies allow.We use them to enforce the Region, to block dangerous actions (leaving the organization, disabling security services), to enforce ownership (only the Network account builds connectivity), and to remove risky shortcuts (root user, long-lived access keys). SCPs say "what's possible", IAM policies say "who can do it".Two rules worth remembering:SCPs never grant permissions. They only limit them.SCPs do not affect the management account, and they do not affect service-linked roles. This is one more reason not to run workloads in the management account.Allow lists vs. deny listsThere are two ways to write SCPs:Allow list: remove FullAWSAccess from the OU and attach an SCP that allows only specific services.Deny list: keep FullAWSAccess and attach SCPs that explicitly deny what you don't want. A common mistake is an allow-list SCP attached next to FullAWSAccess. SCPs on the same OU are combined, so FullAWSAccess already allows everything and only the "Deny" statements have any effect. I made exactly this mistake in my first version. The Workloads OU policy "allowed only ECS, ECR, ELB and logs", but a user in the Dev account could still create SQS queues and work in us-east-1. Worse, a "deny outside Frankfurt" rule that included route53:* broke DNS completely in the Network account. Route 53 is a global service whose API calls are made through us-east-1, so the request never matched eu-central-1:An error occurred (AccessDenied) when calling the ListHostedZones operation: ... is not authorized to perform: route53:ListHostedZones with an explicit deny in a service control policy - Allow lists are also hard to maintain: every new service your team needs requires an SCP change. We use deny lists.We will use four custom policies: → Infrastructure, Deployments, Workloads, Sandbox OUs: Rules every member account must followInfrastructureNetworkManagement → Infrastructure OU: Network account owns connectivity, runs no applications or CI/CD → Deployments OU: Build account owns CI/CD, does not own networking or run applications → Workloads OU: Workload accounts run applications, do not own networking or build imagesWe don't attach our policies to the Security OU. Control Tower manages the Audit and Log Archive accounts and already protects them with its own guardrails (the aws-guardrails-* policies you can see in the list).Every Deny statement below has an exception for the AWSControlTowerExecution role and the stacksets-exec-* roles. Control Tower uses these roles to manage the accounts, and blocking them would break enrollment and drift repair.Policy 1: BaselineGuardrailsGo to AWS Organizations → Policies → Service control policies → Create policy, and name it BaselineGuardrails :{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyLeavingOrganization",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "organizations:LeaveOrganization",
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyRootUser",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "*",
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {"ArnLike": {"aws:PrincipalArn": "arn:aws:iam::*:root"}}
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyLongLivedIamCredentials",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": ["iam:CreateUser", "iam:CreateAccessKey", "iam:CreateLoginProfile"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyDisablingSecurityServices",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "guardduty:DeleteDetector", "guardduty:DisassociateFromAdministratorAccount",
        "guardduty:DisassociateFromMasterAccount", "securityhub:DisableSecurityHub",
        "securityhub:DisassociateFromAdministratorAccount",
        "securityhub:DisassociateFromMasterAccount", "access-analyzer:DeleteAnalyzer"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyOutsideFrankfurt",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "NotAction": [
        "account:*", "acm:*", "aws-marketplace:*", "aws-portal:*", "billing:*", "budgets:*",
        "ce:*", "cloudfront:*", "cur:*", "directconnect:*", "ec2:DescribeRegions",
        "ec2:DescribeTransitGateways", "ec2:DescribeVpnGateways", "ecr-public:*",
        "globalaccelerator:*", "health:*", "iam:*", "kms:*", "networkmanager:*", "organizations:*",
        "pricing:*", "route53:*", "route53domains:*", "s3:GetAccountPublic*",
        "s3:ListAllMyBuckets", "s3:PutAccountPublic*", "shield:*", "sts:*", "support:*",
        "trustedadvisor:*", "waf:*", "wafv2:*"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEquals": {"aws:RequestedRegion": "eu-central-1"},
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
DenyLeavingOrganization — an account cannot remove itself from governance.DenyRootUser — the root user of a member account cannot do anything. Everyday access goes through IAM Identity Center.DenyLongLivedIamCredentials — no IAM users, passwords, or access keys. People use Identity Center, and automation uses IAM roles with temporary credentials (for example, GitHub Actions OIDC).DenyDisablingSecurityServices — once GuardDuty, Security Hub, and IAM Access Analyzer are enabled, nobody in a member account can turn them off or detach from the Audit account.DenyOutsideFrankfurt — everything outside eu-central-1 is denied. Note that this uses NotAction: global services such as IAM, STS, Route 53, CloudFront, Organizations, and billing make their API calls through us-east-1, so they must be excluded or they stop working.Policy 2: InfrastructureNetworkManagement (Infrastructure OU)The Network account owns connectivity. FullAWSAccess already allows that, so we only add what it must not do: run applications or CI/CD.{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyApplicationWorkloads",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "ecs:CreateCluster", "ecs:CreateService", "ecs:RunTask", "eks:CreateCluster",
        "rds:CreateDBInstance", "rds:CreateDBCluster", "apprunner:CreateService",
        "elasticbeanstalk:CreateEnvironment", "lightsail:*"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyBuildResources",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": ["ecr:CreateRepository", "codebuild:CreateProject", "codepipeline:CreatePipeline"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
Policy 3: DeploymentCICDPolicy (Deployments OU)The Build account owns pipelines and container images. It must not create its own network connectivity (Transit Gateway, peering, VPN, Direct Connect) and must not run application services or databases.{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyNetworkConnectivityOwnership",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "ec2:CreateTransitGateway", "ec2:CreateVpcPeeringConnection",
        "ec2:AcceptVpcPeeringConnection", "ec2:CreateVpnGateway", "ec2:CreateCustomerGateway",
        "ec2:CreateVpnConnection", "directconnect:*"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyApplicationRuntime",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "ecs:CreateService", "eks:CreateCluster", "rds:CreateDBInstance", "rds:CreateDBCluster",
        "elasticloadbalancing:CreateLoadBalancer", "apprunner:CreateService",
        "elasticbeanstalk:CreateEnvironment", "lightsail:*"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
Policy 4: WorkloadsECSPolicy (Workloads OU)Dev and Production run the application. They may attach their VPC to a Transit Gateway shared by the Network account, but cannot build their own connectivity, create ECR repositories, push images, or create pipelines. Images are built once in the Build account and only pulled here.{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyNetworkConnectivityOwnership",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "ec2:CreateTransitGateway", "ec2:CreateVpcPeeringConnection",
        "ec2:AcceptVpcPeeringConnection", "ec2:CreateVpnGateway", "ec2:CreateCustomerGateway",
        "ec2:CreateVpnConnection", "directconnect:*"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyBuildResources",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "ecr:CreateRepository", "ecr:PutImage", "ecr:InitiateLayerUpload",
        "codebuild:CreateProject", "codepipeline:CreatePipeline"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": ["arn:aws:iam::*:role/AWSControlTowerExecution", "arn:aws:iam::*:role/stacksets-exec-*"]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
Select the policy → Actions → Attach policy, and choose the OU:BaselineGuardrails → Infrastructure, Deployments, Workloads, SandboxInfrastructureNetworkManagement → InfrastructureDeploymentCICDPolicy → DeploymentsWorkloadsECSPolicy → WorkloadsLeave FullAWSAccess attached. Our policies only take permissions away from it.The final picture for the Workloads OU should be: FullAWSAccess, the Control Tower aws-guardrails-* policy, BaselineGuardrails, and WorkloadsECSPolicy:Always test SCPs right after attaching them. EC2 supports --dry-run, which checks permissions without creating anything:aws ec2 create-transit-gateway --dry-run --profile dev       # UnauthorizedOperation: explicit deny in a service control policy
aws ec2 create-transit-gateway --dry-run --profile network   # DryRunOperation: would have succeeded
The error message names the exact SCP that denied the request, which makes troubleshooting much easier. Section 7 has a script that tests all guardrails at once. before attaching a new SCP to a production OU, attach it to the Sandbox OU or a test account first. A wrong Deny statement affects every account in the OU immediately.6. Network account: shared VPCsWith the guardrails in place, we can build the network. The Network account owns all VPCs, subnets, route tables, NAT gateways, and internet gateways. Dev, Production, and Build don't create VPCs. They receive subnets from the Network account through AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).This model is called VPC sharing:The Network account (owner) creates the VPC and controls routing, NAT, and internet access.The workload account (participant) launches its own resources (ECS tasks, load balancers, databases) into the shared subnets and manages its own security groups.The participant cannot modify or delete the VPC, subnets, route tables, or gateways, even with administrator access in its own account.Why one VPC per environment?One shared VPC for everyone would put Dev and Production on the same network. Instead, each environment gets its own VPC, shared only with its own account, and we don't need a Transit Gateway yet.Before creating anything, plan the IP ranges. VPCs that may need to be connected later (with Transit Gateway or peering) must not overlap. Control Tower created a VPC in every account with the same 172.31.0.0/16 range, and these VPCs can never be connected to each other.We reserve a /16 range for each environment:Each environment VPC uses two Availability Zones and three subnet tiers:Load balancers, NAT gatewayThe app subnets are larger (/20, about 4,000 addresses) because every ECS task on Fargate gets its own IP address. Production uses the same layout with 10.20.x.x. The build VPC needs only two public and two private subnets (10.30.0.0/24, 10.30.1.0/24, 10.30.16.0/20, 10.30.32.0/20).Cleaning up the Control Tower VPCsControl Tower's Account Factory created an empty VPC named aws-controltower-VPC in every new account. We don't need them, so delete them in the Network, Build, Dev, and Production accounts (VPC → Your VPCs → select aws-controltower-VPC → Actions → Delete VPC).To prevent Control Tower from creating these VPCs in future accounts, go to Control Tower → Account Factory → Network configuration → Edit, and set the maximum number of private subnets to 0.Sign in to the Network account, open VPC → Create VPC, and choose VPC and more. This wizard creates the VPC, subnets, route tables, internet gateway, NAT gateway, and VPC endpoints in one step.Use the following settings:Name tag auto-generation: devIPv4 CIDR block: 10.10.0.0/16, no IPv6Number of Availability Zones: 2 (eu-central-1a and eu-central-1b)Number of public subnets: 2Number of private subnets: 4Expand Customize subnets CIDR blocks and enter the CIDRs from the table above. The wizard's defaults would give the public subnets /20 ranges, which wastes addresses we want to keep for ECS tasks.ECS tasks in private subnets need outbound access to pull container images, send logs, and call external APIs. This is the job of the NAT gateway. The wizard offers three options:None — no outbound internet access from private subnetsZonal — a NAT gateway in a specific AZ, either "In 1 AZ" or "1 per AZ"Regional — a single NAT gateway that AWS automatically makes available across your AZs, without managing a NAT gateway and route per AZWe make a different choice per environment:: Zonal, in 1 AZ. Cheapest. If one AZ fails, dev loses outbound access temporarily, which is acceptable.: Regional. Highly available across AZs without managing one NAT gateway per AZ: Zonal, in 1 AZ. CI/CD can tolerate a short outageFor prod, choose Regional instead of Zonal. There is no AZ selection for a regional NAT gateway, because AWS manages its availability across zones:Choose S3 Gateway. Gateway endpoints are free. Amazon ECR stores image layers in S3, so most of the traffic when ECS pulls an image goes through the endpoint instead of the NAT gateway, which reduces NAT data processing costs.We don't create interface endpoints (ECR, CloudWatch Logs, STS) yet. At about $0.01 per hour per AZ each, five of them in two AZs cost more than the NAT gateway they would replace.Keep DNS hostnames and DNS resolution enabled.Before creating the VPC, check the Preview panel on the right. It shows every resource the wizard will create. You should see six subnets, one internet gateway, one NAT gateway, and private route tables with a 0.0.0.0/0 route to the NAT gateway.Click Create VPC. Creating the NAT gateway takes a minute or two.Open the VPC and look at the Resource map tab. It shows the whole design on one screen: six subnets in two AZs, the public route table pointing to the internet gateway, and the private route tables pointing to the NAT gateway and the S3 endpoint.Repeat the same steps for prod (10.20.0.0/16, Regional NAT gateway) and build (10.30.0.0/16, 2 public and 2 private subnets, Zonal NAT gateway in 1 AZ).First, allow RAM to share resources within the organization. In the management account, open Resource Access Manager → Settings and enable Enable sharing with AWS Organizations. Without this setting, resources can only be shared through invitations.Now, in the Network account, open Resource Access Manager → Resource shares → Create resource share:Resource type: Subnets. Select the six dev subnetsPrincipals: leave "Allow sharing with principals outside your organization" disabled, choose AWS account, and enter the Dev account IDCreate two more shares the same way:prod-network — the six prod subnets, shared with the Production accountbuild-network — only the two build private subnets, shared with the Build account. The Build account doesn't need public subnets, because the SCP denies it from creating load balancers anyway.Troubleshooting: "can only be shared within your AWS Organization"When I created the first share, it failed with this error, even though organization sharing was enabled a few minutes earlier:The message suggests waiting for onboarding to finish, but mine still failed after 40 minutes, from every account. The real hint came from the CLI: sharing with the organization as principal returned UnknownResourceException: Organization o-xxxxxxxx. RAM had never imported the organization, even though the Organizations console listed RAM as a trusted service.The fix was to reset RAM trusted access from the management account:aws organizations disable-aws-service-access --service-principal ram.amazonaws.com
aws ram enable-sharing-with-aws-organization
Run this only before you have any resource shares. Disabling trusted access affects existing organization-based shares. After the reset, sharing worked within a minute.Before that, I ruled out our own SCP by detaching BaselineGuardrails from the Infrastructure OU for a minute. A good habit in a governed account: rule out your own SCPs first. The error message names the denying SCP, as in this example where the region lock blocks CloudTrail in us-east-1:User: arn:aws:sts::111122223333:assumed-role/AWSReservedSSO_AWSAdministratorAccess_0123456789abcdef/admin
is not authorized to perform: cloudtrail:LookupEvents with an explicit deny in a service control policy:
arn:aws:organizations::444455556666:policy/o-exampleorgid/service_control_policy/p-examplepolicy
Verifying from the Dev accountSign in to the Dev account and open VPC → Subnets. The shared subnets appear with the Network account as the owner.Two things may look surprising here:The subnets have no names. Tags are not shared with participant accounts, but you can add your own Name tags in the Dev account.AZ names can differ between accounts: "eu-central-1a" may be a different physical AZ elsewhere. Compare AZ IDs (for example, euc1-az2), not names.The Dev account can now launch ECS services and load balancers into these subnets, but it cannot change routing or create its own internet path.7. Verify your landing zoneGuardrails are easy to get subtly wrong, so I keep a script that checks the important rules in one run. It only uses read-only calls and EC2 --dry-run, so it never creates anything:#!/usr/bin/env bash
check() { # name, expected (allow|deny), command...
  local name=$1 expect=$2; shift 2
  out=$("$@" 2>&1); rc=$?
  if echo "$out" | grep -qE 'explicit deny|UnauthorizedOperation|AccessDenied'; then got=deny
  elif echo "$out" | grep -q 'DryRunOperation' || [ $rc -eq 0 ]; then got=allow
  else got="error: $(echo "$out" | tail -1)"; fi
  [ "$got" = "$expect" ] && s=OK || s=FAIL
  printf '%-4s %-48s expected=%-5s got=%s\n' "$s" "$name" "$expect" "$got"
}
check "network: Route 53 works"                 allow aws route53 list-hosted-zones --profile network
check "dev: eu-central-1 allowed"               allow aws ec2 describe-vpcs --profile dev
check "dev: us-east-1 denied (region lock)"     deny  aws ec2 describe-vpcs --region us-east-1 --profile dev
check "dev: IAM (global) still works"           allow aws iam list-roles --max-items 1 --profile dev
check "dev: cannot create Transit Gateway"      deny  aws ec2 create-transit-gateway --dry-run --profile dev
check "build: cannot create Transit Gateway"    deny  aws ec2 create-transit-gateway --dry-run --profile build
check "network: can create Transit Gateway"     allow aws ec2 create-transit-gateway --dry-run --profile network
check "prod: us-west-2 denied (region lock)"    deny  aws ec2 describe-vpcs --region us-west-2 --profile prod
Output from my organization:OK   network: Route 53 works                          expected=allow got=allow
OK   dev: eu-central-1 allowed                        expected=allow got=allow
OK   dev: us-east-1 denied (region lock)              expected=deny  got=deny
OK   dev: IAM (global) still works                    expected=allow got=allow
OK   dev: cannot create Transit Gateway               expected=deny  got=deny
OK   build: cannot create Transit Gateway             expected=deny  got=deny
OK   network: can create Transit Gateway              expected=allow got=allow
OK   prod: us-west-2 denied (region lock)             expected=deny  got=deny
The "allow" checks matter too: a guardrail that blocks too much is also a bug, like my first region lock that broke Route 53. Run the script after every SCP change.8. Troubleshooting summaryEvery problem I hit while building this part, in one place:Route 53 returns AccessDenied with "explicit deny in a service control policy": A region-lock SCP denied route53:*. Route 53 calls go through us-east-1. Fix: Use NotAction and exclude global services (section 5).An "allow only ECS, ECR, ..." SCP doesn't restrict anything: FullAWSAccess is still attached, and SCPs on the same target are combined. Fix: Keep FullAWSAccess and write Deny statements (section 5).RAM: "can only be shared within your AWS Organization", still failing after 40 minutes: RAM never imported the organization (UnknownResourceException for the org ID). Fix: Disable and re-enable RAM trusted access before creating shares (section 6).Shared subnets have no names in the participant account: Tags aren't shared. Fix: Identify subnets by CIDR or AZ ID, and tag them locally if needed.If you built this only to learn, delete it in this order. Participants must remove their resources from shared subnets before the owner can delete them.Dev, Production, and Build accounts: delete anything you launched into the shared subnets, including security groups.Network account: delete the three RAM shares. Then delete the NAT gateways and release their Elastic IP addresses (this stops most of the cost), and delete the three VPCs.Management account: detach and delete the four custom SCPs. To remove everything, decommission the landing zone in Control Tower, then close the member accounts. Closed accounts stay suspended for 90 days.10. Conclusion and what's nextWe now have a landing zone where every account has one clear job, and the boundaries are enforced rather than just documented:SCPs make each account's "must not" rules hold, even for administrators. We tested every rule from the CLI.The Network account owns all routing and egress. Each workload account receives only the subnets of its own environment, and it cannot change them.No IAM users and no long-lived keys exist anywhere. All access goes through IAM Identity Center.In part 2, we will set up the Build account on this foundation: CodeCommit without IAM users, an ECR repository that only Dev and Production can pull from, and CodeBuild inside the shared VPC, with its own set of surprises.

This is an example, not a report from a customer. Imagine a support inbox: someone never asked for a password reset, but they received a "your password was changed" email, and now their login no longer works. Their old password was long and unique. Nobody guessed it.Nobody needed to. A reset flow is a way into an account without the current password, by design. A valid reset link may let whoever holds it set a new password. Whether that is enough to take the account depends on other controls: a second factor at the reset step, a prompt to re-authenticate, a notification the real owner sees in time, session invalidation after the change, or a token that has already expired or been used.OWASP calls this functionality "a common source of vulnerabilities" even though it "looks straightforward and easy to implement" (OWASP Forgot Password Cheat Sheet). The usual weak points sit at six places in the flow. Run the active tests only on systems you own or have written permission to test.1. The request should say the same thing to everyoneWhen someone types an address into "Forgot password", the response should not reveal whether an account exists. OWASP's Authentication Cheat Sheet lists "We just sent you a password reset link" and "This email address doesn't exist in our database" as incorrect responses, and gives the correct one: "If that email address is in our database, we will send you an email to reset your password" (OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet).The message is the easy part. The Forgot Password Cheat Sheet also asks for responses that "return in a consistent amount of time," for example by sending the email asynchronously instead of exiting early for unknown addresses. A page that answers in a few milliseconds for unknown addresses and noticeably later for real ones tells an attacker the same thing as a different message.Then rate-limit the endpoint. OWASP recommends per-account rate limiting or a CAPTCHA, because otherwise an attacker can flood a user's inbox. Return the same  whether or not the account exists (RFC 6585, section 4), or the limiter itself becomes an enumeration oracle. Do not lock the account: OWASP notes that a forgotten-password attack should not produce a lockout, because that lets anyone deny access to users whose names they know.2. Build the link from configuration, never from the requestMany frameworks make it easy to build an absolute URL from "the current host". In a reset email, that convenience is the vulnerability. PortSwigger describes password reset poisoning: an attacker submits a reset request for the victim with a Host header pointing at a domain they control, the site sends a genuine token, and the link inside points at the attacker. If the victim clicks it, or a mail scanner fetches it, the token is delivered to the attacker (PortSwigger: Password reset poisoning).OWASP's instruction is direct: "Don't rely on the Host header while creating the reset URLs." Use one configured HTTPS base URL per environment, and reject host names you do not serve. Treat  the same way: trust it only from your own proxy.3. The token: random, long, hashed, single-use, short-livedOWASP lists the properties every reset token needs: generated with a cryptographically secure random number generator, long enough to resist brute force, linked to an individual user, invalidated after use, stored securely, and expiring after an appropriate period.Here is a minimal sketch in Python that follows those properties. It is something to adapt, not a drop-in library.import hashlib, secrets, time

TOKEN_TTL = 30 * 60
BASE_URL = "https://shop.example"  # from configuration, never from the request
_store = {}  # token_hash -> {"user_id", "expires", "used"}; use your database

def _hash(token):
    return hashlib.sha256(token.encode()).hexdigest()

def issue_reset_link(user_id, now=None):
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    for rec in _store.values():
        if rec["user_id"] == user_id:
            rec["used"] = True
    token = secrets.token_urlsafe(32)
    _store[_hash(token)] = {"user_id": user_id, "expires": now + TOKEN_TTL, "used": False}
    return f"{BASE_URL}/reset?token={token}"

def redeem(token, now=None):
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    if not isinstance(token, str) or not token:
        return None
    rec = _store.get(_hash(token))
    if rec is None or rec["used"] or now > rec["expires"]:
        return None
    rec["used"] = True
    return rec["user_id"]
secrets.token_urlsafe(32) uses Python's module for security tokens, which documents 32 bytes (256 bits) of randomness as sufficient for the typical case (Python docs: secrets). Never use , timestamps, user IDs or sequential values. Store only a hash, so a leaked database does not contain working links. For a long random token, a fast hash such as SHA-256 is a reasonable choice; slow password hashing is meant for secrets people choose (OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet). Issuing a new token should burn older ones for that user. An expired, used or unknown token should produce the same generic error page.The sketch is single-process and in-memory. In a real application, two requests can redeem the same token at once, so marking it used must be one atomic database operation that succeeds only if it changes exactly one unused, unexpired row.4. Keep the token where you put itThe token sits in a URL, and URLs travel.OWASP asks reset pages to send Referrer-Policy: no-referrer. When no policy is set, browsers default to strict-origin-when-cross-origin, which sends only the origin on cross-origin requests (MDN: Referrer-Policy). A missing header is not proof that the token leaks to third parties in current browsers. It is a missing layer: the default still sends the full URL on same-origin requests, and a looser policy elsewhere on the site would apply.Analytics, chat widgets and session-replay tools on the reset page can read . The cleanest pattern is a reset page with no third-party scripts. Mail scanners can also open the link before the user does. If a GET burns the single-use token, the real user may find it "already used". Show the form on GET, and consume the token only on POST.5. After the reset, end the old sessionsIf an attacker already had a session, a new password alone does not remove them. OWASP recommends invalidating existing sessions, or asking the user whether to do so, and advises against signing the user in automatically after the reset. The Session Management Cheat Sheet lists a completed password change among the events that should require reauthentication (OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet).Send a notification that the password was changed, without the password in it, as OWASP recommends. In the example above, that email is what turned a silent change into a support ticket. Include a way to report "this wasn't me" that does not depend on the account's current password.How to test your own flowUse two test accounts you control, and staging where possible. We have not run the commands below against a live site.Submit the form for a real test address and for an address that does not exist, and compare status, visible message, size and timing. You want the same status and message for both. A "no such user" error is a leak. Small size differences can come from a CSRF token; timing needs many requests before it means anything.In staging, send the same request with a foreign  (and again with ) and read the email that arrives. If the link points at the foreign host, that header is being trusted. A  or  from the proxy only shows that this request was refused; read the link-building code as well.With a test account, reuse a spent link, use the older of two links, wait past expiry, and open the page with GET before submitting the form. The last check shows whether a GET burns the token. Look at the reset page for  and third-party scripts. After a reset, a second browser that was already signed in should be signed out or asked to authenticate again.If every step behaved as intended for these test accounts, this flow did what you asked when you tested it. Other recovery paths, and the difference between staging and production, still need their own review.We are the HackerMe team, and we build a free scanner for public pages and headers. It cannot read reset emails or token storage.

{
  "Attributes": {
    "ApproximateNumberOfMessages": "4819",
    "ApproximateNumberOfMessagesNotVisible": "0"
  }
}
That was the output of aws sqs get-queue-attributes on prod-merchant-payouts-dlq when our finance lead finally asked why Tuesday's Stripe Connect batch was missing  in disbursements.Every single REST API contract test in our monorepo had passed that morning. Our Playwright suite was green. Our Go service's unit tests were green. Our TypeScript worker's unit tests were green.None of them caught the bug, because the bug did not live on an HTTP route. It lived in the invisible gap between a Go service calling  and a Node.js worker calling JSON.parse(record.Body) as PayoutRequestedEvent.Why REST Contract Tests Miss Half Your ArchitectureEngineering teams spend huge amounts of energy locking down synchronous HTTP boundaries. We generate TypeScript clients from OpenAPI specs, run Supertest against Express routers, and fail CI if a response field changes from  to .Then we take an asynchronous SQS queue, a Kafka topic, or an internal SNS fan-out—which often carries the actual money-moving side effects—and treat it like a lawless JSON dropbox.In our case, two separate teams owned the two ends of :): Maintained by the core billing team in Go.): Maintained by the platform team in TypeScript/Node 22.In , the consumer had a type annotation that looked safe in code review and meant literally nothing at runtime:// Looks typed in your IDE. Completely unchecked at runtime.
const payload = JSON.parse(message.Body!) as {
  merchantId: string;
  payoutCents: number;
  currency: string;
};
When  refactored its decimal serialization to avoid float rounding and started emitting  as a string instead of  as an integer,  happily returned a string. Downstream, Math.round(payload.payoutCents) did not throw—Wait: actually,  works in JavaScript!What  throw was the strict Postgres  / Stripe SDK validation two functions later (Invalid integer: "14900.00" when a fractional fee was included), crashing the worker, exhausting , and dumping 4,819 messages into prod-merchant-payouts-dlq.Nobody was watching the DLQ metric in Datadog because that queue had sat at  for eight months.What Unit Tests Lied About on Both SidesWhen we pulled the two repos side-by-side after replaying the DLQ, both teams had written tests for that exact flow: asserted that its  helper produced valid JSON containing . It passed. had a Jest test with a hardcoded fixture file fixtures/payout-message.json where  was still . It passed too.Static fixtures in consumer unit tests are a slow-acting poison. The day you write fixtures/payout-message.json, it matches reality. Six months later, after three producer refactors, your consumer tests are certifying a payload shape that no longer exists anywhere in production.One Shared JSON Schema Contract That Breaks Both Repos.We did not want to deploy a heavy Confluent Schema Registry just for 14 SQS queues. We wanted something boring that worked across Go and TypeScript inside GitHub Actions with zero new infrastructure.So we made JSON Schema (Draft 2020-12) the single source of truth inside a lightweight shared directory (contracts/queues/*.schema.json), paired with:Runtime validation at the consumer boundary using  (or  compiled to JSON Schema) so malformed messages fail  at line 1 of the handler with a structured  log instead of half-way through a Stripe call.A CI cross-repo contract check so if  changes a struct tag or field type, it has to run against the exact same  file that  consumes.Here is contracts/queues/payout-requested.v1.schema.json:{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://contracts.internal/queues/payout-requested.v1.schema.json",
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": ["eventId", "merchantId", "payoutCents", "currency", "occurredAt"],
  "properties": {
    "eventId": { "type": "string", "format": "uuid" },
    "merchantId": { "type": "string", "minLength": 4 },
    "payoutCents": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1 },
    "currency": { "type": "string", "enum": ["USD", "EUR", "GBP"] },
    "occurredAt": { "type": "string", "format": "date-time" }
  }
}
And here is the TypeScript consumer boundary wrapper (src/queues/validateMessage.ts) that replaced :import Ajv from 'ajv';
import addFormats from 'ajv-formats';
import payoutSchema from '../../contracts/queues/payout-requested.v1.schema.json';

const ajv = new Ajv({ allErrors: true, strict: true });
addFormats(ajv);

const validatePayout = ajv.compile<{
  eventId: string;
  merchantId: string;
  payoutCents: number;
  currency: 'USD' | 'EUR' | 'GBP';
  occurredAt: string;
}>(payoutSchema);

export function parsePayoutMessage(rawBody: string | undefined) {
  if (!rawBody) {
    throw new Error('SQS_EMPTY_BODY: Missing message body');
  }

  const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(rawBody);
  if (!validatePayout(parsed)) {
    const details = ajv.errorsText(validatePayout.errors);
    throw new Error(`SCHEMA_DRIFT_ERROR: ${details}`);
  }

  return parsed;
}
Notice additionalProperties: false and  on . If  ever serializes  as a string again,  rejects it in under 0.1 milliseconds with an unmistakable  instead of letting it corrupt downstream state.Testing the Real SQS Loop With LocalStack in CI.Validating a schema in memory is only half the story. You also want to prove that the Go producer's  survive SQS attributes, base64/JSON encoding, and the TypeScript consumer's  function.We added a 55-line integration test (tests/contracts/sqs-payout-contract.test.ts) that spins up LocalStack via Testcontainers (or a GitHub Actions service container), runs the Go producer binary's  command to push real messages onto a local queue, and lets the TypeScript consumer pull and validate them:import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';
import { SQSClient, CreateQueueCommand, ReceiveMessageCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-sqs';
import { parsePayoutMessage } from '../../src/queues/validateMessage';

const sqs = new SQSClient({
  region: 'us-east-1',
  endpoint: process.env.LOCALSTACK_URL || 'http://127.0.0.1:4566',
  credentials: { accessKeyId: 'test', secretAccessKey: 'test' },
});

test('Go producer payload passes TypeScript SQS consumer contract', async () => {
  const { QueueUrl } = await sqs.send(
    new CreateQueueCommand({ QueueName: `payouts-contract-${Date.now()}` })
  );

  // Run the actual Go producer serialization path against LocalStack
  execFileSync('./bin/ledger-emit-sample', ['--queue-url', QueueUrl!], {
    env: { ...process.env, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL: process.env.LOCALSTACK_URL || 'http://127.0.0.1:4566' },
  });

  const received = await sqs.send(
    new ReceiveMessageCommand({
      QueueUrl,
      MaxNumberOfMessages: 1,
      WaitTimeSeconds: 3,
    })
  );

  expect(received.Messages).toHaveLength(1);
  const validated = parsePayoutMessage(received.Messages![0].Body);
  expect(validated.payoutCents).toBeGreaterThan(0);
});
That single test runs in  in GitHub Actions. No mocked JSON fixtures. If a Go developer changes  to  on —even by adding  to a Go struct tag—that test blows up on their PR before  ever sees it in production.Never Let a Dead-Letter Queue Sit Silent AgainThe other embarrassing half of the $62K incident was not technical—it was operational. A queue had 4,819 failed payout messages sitting in it for 19 hours because nobody had wired an alarm to ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible > 0 on .We fixed that across all 14 queues in one Terraform module (infra/modules/sqs_dlq_alarm/main.tf) so every new queue gets a PagerDuty/Slack alert on  in the DLQ, not message #4,819:resource "aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm" "dlq_not_empty" {
  for_each            = toset(var.dlq_names)
  alarm_name          = "${each.value}-has-messages"
  comparison_operator = "GreaterThanThreshold"
  evaluation_periods  = 1
  metric_name         = "ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible"
  namespace           = "AWS/SQS"
  period              = 60
  statistic           = "Maximum"
  threshold           = 0
  treat_missing_data  = "notBreaching"

  dimensions = {
    QueueName = each.value
  }

  alarm_actions = [var.ops_alerts_sns_topic_arn]
}
Zero tolerance on DLQ depth sounds aggressive until you realize a dead-letter queue is literally a bucket of failed customer promises. If one message lands there, you want to know within 60 seconds, not when finance reconciles Stripe payouts on Wednesday morning.Handling In-Flight Messages During Schema UpgradesPutting a strict JSON Schema validator on a queue consumer introduces a subtle trap during rolling deployments: .When Kubernetes rolls  to a new version, there can easily be 800 messages still sitting in SQS serialized by the  version of . If you rename a required field or tighten an enum in payout-requested.v1.schema.json, your new consumer pods will immediately reject valid in-flight messages as .We learned that the hard way on our second schema update. Now we enforce two rules in CI: schema in a breaking way. Adding an optional property is fine. Removing a field, changing a type, or adding a new  key means creating payout-requested.v2.schema.json and supporting both  and  in the consumer until  traffic drains to zero.Automate the backwards-compatibility check. We added a 45-line CI script (scripts/check-schema-backcompat.ts) that diffs every modified  file against  and fails the PR if a  field was added or a property's  changed:import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';
import fs from 'node:fs';

interface JsonSchema {
  required?: string[];
  properties?: Record<string, { type?: string }>;
}

const changedFiles = execFileSync(
  'git',
  ['diff', '--name-only', 'origin/main...HEAD', '--', 'contracts/queues/*.schema.json'],
  { encoding: 'utf8' }
)
  .trim()
  .split('\n')
  .filter(Boolean);

for (const file of changedFiles) {
  let oldRaw = '';
  try {
    oldRaw = execFileSync('git', ['show', `origin/main:${file}`], { encoding: 'utf8' });
  } catch {
    // Brand new schema version (e.g., v2) — always allowed
    continue;
  }

  const prev = JSON.parse(oldRaw) as JsonSchema;
  const next = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8')) as JsonSchema;

  const prevReq = new Set(prev.required || []);
  for (const key of next.required || []) {
    if (!prevReq.has(key)) {
      console.error(`BREAKING: Added required field "${key}" to ${file}. Bump to a new version instead.`);
      process.exit(1);
    }
  }

  for (const [prop, def] of Object.entries(prev.properties || {})) {
    const nextDef = next.properties?.[prop];
    if (!nextDef || nextDef.type !== def.type) {
      console.error(`BREAKING: Property "${prop}" changed or removed in ${file}. Bump schema version.`);
      process.exit(1);
    }
  }
}

console.log('All modified queue schemas are backwards-compatible.');
Replaying 4,819 Dead-Letter Messages Without Double-PayingOne last thing nobody tells you until you actually have to drain a 4,819-message financial DLQ: you cannot just click "Start DLQ Redrive" in the AWS Console and walk away.During the 19-hour outage, our finance team had already manually wired payouts for 140 urgent enterprise merchants via the Stripe dashboard. If we had blindly redriven all 4,819 messages back into the main queue, those 140 merchants would have been paid twice.Before replaying a single message, we added an INSERT INTO processed_queue_events (event_id) VALUES ($1) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING idempotency guard backed by Postgres, pre-seeded it with the 140 manually settled s, and ran a small redrive script that patched  from string to integer on the fly while logging every skipped duplicate.We tested the redrive script with  on the first 50 DLQ messages, verified the Stripe idempotency keys matched, and then drained the remaining 4,769 messages in batches of 100. The whole replay finished in 4 minutes and 40 seconds across 20 concurrent workers, with zero double payouts and zero manual spreadsheet reconciliation afterward.That idempotency table stayed in  permanently—because SQS is at-least-once delivery anyway, and sooner or later every distributed queue delivers the same message twice.The Quiet Side Effect Nobody MentionsOnce we put JSON Schema contracts and LocalStack round-trip tests on our SQS queues, we expected fewer production outages. And yes, we have had  schema-drift DLQ incidents in the five months since.What surprised me was how much faster onboarding got. New engineers stopped having to  through three Go repositories to figure out what shape an SQS event actually had—they just opened contracts/queues/*.schema.json and read the exact contract in ten seconds.If your test suite checks every REST route twice while your SQS, Kafka, or BullMQ workers still rely on JSON.parse(...) as MyType and hand-written test fixtures, you are only testing half your system. The other half is one struct tag away from a very quiet, very expensive pileup.

At 11:40 AM on a Monday, nine engineers were sitting in a Slack thread watching a progress bar that had not moved in 45 minutes.Someone had merged a half-baked Alembic migration into  and locked the  table. Meanwhile, a real  bug on  was actively hitting paying customers in production, and our hotfix could not ship because company policy said every release had to pass a full QA sign-off on staging first.Four hours later, the hotfix finally went out. Nobody felt safer. Worse, when we checked our incident log that week, we noticed something embarrassing: staging had failed to catch four of our last five production outages anyway.Why? Because staging had 120 clean seed rows in Postgres, zero concurrent webhook traffic from Stripe, and a Redis instance that never evicted a key. Production had 18 million rows, spiky Monday morning batch jobs, and weird legacy payloads from 2021.We were paying  in AWS bills to test against a fantasy world.Two months later, we turned off the shared staging cluster for good.Why Shared Staging Always Turns Into a BottleneckIf your team has more than five developers, a shared staging environment rots in predictable ways: Developer A runs a destructive migration for Feature X; Developer B's API tests immediately fail for reasons that have nothing to do with their branch. Someone manually edits a feature flag or deletes a test tenant during a demo, and three automated suites go red until someone resets the database. Queries that finish in 4 milliseconds on 120 staging rows do a full sequential scan and time out at 30 seconds against 18 million production rows.We still run fast unit tests, contract checks, and ephemeral per-PR Docker Compose smoke tests in GitHub Actions (those cost us about $410/month now). What we killed was the long-lived, shared "pre-production gate" where QA engineers spent half their week babysitting stale seed data.Instead, we moved real verification into production behind three guardrails: progressive traffic splitting, dark-launch shadow traffic, and isolated synthetic probes.Guardrail 1: Argo Rollouts With Automated Prometheus RollbacksIf you test in production without automatic blast-radius control, you are not doing shift-right testing—you are just breaking things faster.We replaced our standard Kubernetes  manifests with . When a new container image ships, it gets  for three minutes. An  polls Prometheus every 30 seconds. If the 5xx error rate crosses 0.5% or p99 latency jumps above 650ms, Argo aborts the rollout and drains the canary pods in about 45 seconds without a human touching anything.Here is the exact  and  pair we use for our  service:apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: AnalysisTemplate
metadata:
  name: canary-slo-check
spec:
  args:
    - name: service-name
  metrics:
    - name: http-5xx-rate
      interval: 30s
      failureLimit: 2
      successCondition: result[0] <= 0.005
      provider:
        prometheus:
          address: http://prometheus-operated.monitoring:9090
          query: |
            sum(rate(http_requests_total{service="{{args.service-name}}",status=~"5.."}[2m]))
            /
            sum(rate(http_requests_total{service="{{args.service-name}}"}[2m]))
    - name: p99-latency-ms
      interval: 30s
      failureLimit: 2
      successCondition: result[0] <= 650
      provider:
        prometheus:
          address: http://prometheus-operated.monitoring:9090
          query: |
            histogram_quantile(0.99, sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{service="{{args.service-name}}"}[2m])) by (le)) * 1000
---
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Rollout
metadata:
  name: exports-api
spec:
  replicas: 6
  strategy:
    canary:
      steps:
        - setWeight: 5
        - pause: { duration: 3m }
        - analysis:
            templates:
              - templateName: canary-slo-check
            args:
              - name: service-name
                value: exports-api-canary
        - setWeight: 25
        - pause: { duration: 5m }
        - analysis:
            templates:
              - templateName: canary-slo-check
            args:
              - name: service-name
                value: exports-api-canary
        - setWeight: 100
Notice the two separate pauses: 3 minutes at 5% traffic, then 5 minutes at 25%. Most memory leaks and bad database query plans show up right around the 25% mark when connection pools start filling up.Guardrail 2: Shadow Traffic for Risky Read and Pricing PathsCanaries are great, but what if you rewrote a pricing calculator or a search query planner and do not want  to see a wrong number?For read-only and idempotent endpoints, we mirror live production HTTP requests to the new version in the background using Envoy/Nginx traffic shadowing. The user gets the response from the old stable pods. Meanwhile, the canary pod processes the exact same real-world JSON payload, headers, and edge-case query strings—and we compare the status codes and latency in Datadog.Here is the Nginx Ingress annotation block we used before migrating to Envoy:location /api/v1/pricing/quote {
    proxy_pass http://pricing_stable;
    mirror /_shadow_pricing;
    mirror_request_body on;
}

location = /_shadow_pricing {
    internal;
    proxy_pass http://pricing_canary$request_uri;
    proxy_set_header X-Shadow-Traffic "true";
    proxy_connect_timeout 200ms;
    proxy_read_timeout 500ms;
}
Two hard-won lessons on shadow traffic: or  endpoints unless your downstream handler checks  and skips writing to Stripe or sending customer emails. We learned to put an explicit middleware guard at the top of our Express router that rejects  on non-GET routes unless the handler opts in.Set aggressive timeouts (). Nginx's  directive sends the subrequest asynchronously, so a slow canary does not normally block the user,  if you exhaust worker connections during a spike. Short timeouts keep the shadow pool from starving the primary pool.Shadow traffic caught a bug in our search query builder that staging never saw in three weeks: real customers were passing 40+ comma-separated filter tags in a single URL, blowing past an internal regex limit and throwing  errors.Guardrail 3: Tenant-Isolated Synthetic Probes in ProductionShadow traffic tests existing endpoints, and canaries test real user flows. Neither one tests a brand-new multi-step workflow (like onboarding a new workspace or generating a PDF export) before you flip the feature flag on for customers.That is where our synthetic canary probe comes in.We created one dedicated internal tenant in production (tenant_id: "canary_synthetic_01"), excluded from all revenue and analytics rollups in Snowflake. Every 60 seconds, a lightweight Kubernetes  runs a Playwright/API probe against production with .If we are rolling out a new billing flow behind a LaunchDarkly/Unleash feature flag, we turn that flag on  for  first. The probe exercises the real production database, real Redis cluster, and real S3 bucket—then cleans up its own test artifacts at the end of the run.Here is the TypeScript probe script () that runs on that 60-second loop:import { strict as assert } from 'node:assert';

const PROD_BASE_URL = process.env.PROD_BASE_URL || 'https://api.example.com';
const CANARY_TOKEN = process.env.CANARY_SYNTHETIC_TOKEN!;

async function runCanaryProbe() {
  const start = Date.now();

  // 1. Trigger a real export job under the isolated synthetic tenant
  const createRes = await fetch(`${PROD_BASE_URL}/api/v1/exports`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      Authorization: `Bearer ${CANARY_TOKEN}`,
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      'x-canary-probe': 'true',
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      format: 'csv',
      dateRangeDays: 7,
    }),
  });

  assert.equal(createRes.status, 202, `Expected 202 from /exports, got ${createRes.status}`);
  const { exportId } = (await createRes.json()) as { exportId: string };

  // 2. Poll until the worker finishes or 15s budget expires
  let status = 'pending';
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 15; attempt++) {
    const pollRes = await fetch(`${PROD_BASE_URL}/api/v1/exports/${exportId}`, {
      headers: {
        Authorization: `Bearer ${CANARY_TOKEN}`,
        'x-canary-probe': 'true',
      },
    });
    assert.equal(pollRes.status, 200, `Poll failed with ${pollRes.status}`);
    const data = (await pollRes.json()) as { status: string; downloadUrl?: string };
    status = data.status;

    if (status === 'completed') {
      assert.ok(data.downloadUrl, 'Completed export missing S3 downloadUrl');
      break;
    }
    if (status === 'failed') {
      throw new Error(`Export job ${exportId} failed in production worker`);
    }
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 1000));
  }

  assert.equal(status, 'completed', `Export ${exportId} timed out in status=${status}`);

  // 3. Delete the synthetic artifact so S3 and Postgres stay clean
  await fetch(`${PROD_BASE_URL}/api/v1/exports/${exportId}`, {
    method: 'DELETE',
    headers: {
      Authorization: `Bearer ${CANARY_TOKEN}`,
      'x-canary-probe': 'true',
    },
  });

  console.log(`Canary probe passed in ${Date.now() - start}ms (exportId=${exportId})`);
}

runCanaryProbe().catch((err) => {
  console.error('CANARY_PROBE_FAILED:', err);
  process.exit(1);
});
Two consecutive failures from that  page the on-call engineer  trigger an automatic feature-flag kill-switch via webhook.What We Still Test Before Production (No, We Didn't Delete Unit Tests)Every time I tell someone we killed staging, they assume we just  straight to  and pray.Not even close. We actually became  about pre-merge checks in CI: Every migration runs in an ephemeral Postgres container in GitHub Actions against a scrubbed schema snapshot, plus a lock-timeout check () so nobody accidentally locks  for 45 minutes again. Fast, deterministic, zero shared state. Runs in under 3 minutes per PR. Frontend PRs still spin up a lightweight Vercel/preview build pointed at read-only mock or ephemeral backends for visual review—no shared database required.What disappeared was the 4-hour human staging queue where nine engineers waited for one brittle cluster to unfreeze.The Two Times Production Testing Bit UsMoving verification to production is not free. Two mistakes in the first month taught us where the sharp edges are:Synthetic traffic leaked into an async billing job. Our API gateway filtered out  from analytics, but the background SQS export worker did not read HTTP headers—it only saw the  in the job payload. By Friday afternoon,  had generated 43,200 fake export rows in our customer usage table. The fix was simple: we moved the synthetic marker into the JWT claims and tenant metadata () so every downstream worker and dbt model ignores it automatically.A column drop broke the 95% stable pods. During a canary rollout, old pods and new pods share the same Postgres database at the same time. Someone merged a PR that renamed  to  in a single migration. The 5% canary pods were happy; the 95% old pods immediately started throwing s. Now we enforce a strict two-PR  rule in CI: PR #1 adds the new column and dual-writes, and PR #2 drops the old column only after 100% of pods are on the new version.A noisy third-party webhook masked a real canary regression. During a Monday afternoon Stripe retry storm, our canary  spiked to 0.7% because of upstream s, triggering an unnecessary rollback on a harmless CSS/BFF deploy. We fixed that by splitting Prometheus  queries into  vs , and excluding known third-party  upstream timeouts from the canary abort budget. Once we separated our own bugs from Stripe's hiccups, false-positive rollbacks dropped to near zero.Six Months Without Staging: The Honest ScorecardShutting down our shared staging cluster was not magic. The first three weeks were bumpy—we had to train developers to wrap every risky change in a feature flag and add  filtering to our billing reports so synthetic runs didn't inflate customer usage metrics.Once that settled, though, the numbers spoke for themselves:Monthly non-prod AWS bill: Dropped from  to  (ephemeral CI runners only).Hotfix time-to-production: Cut from  (waiting on staging locks and manual QA sign-off) to .Customer-facing P1 outages: Dropped from 5 in the previous six months to  (and Argo Rollouts auto-reverted that one at 5% traffic in 48 seconds before support even got a ticket).If your staging environment costs thousands of dollars a month, blocks releases every Monday, and still lets real bugs slip into production, stop trying to make staging look more like production. It never will. Put your safety nets where the real traffic actually lives. Start with one non-critical service, wire up a 5% Argo canary with a two-metric Prometheus check, and see how fast your team stops missing Monday staging freezes.

I could see how long the model call took in an OpenTelemetry span. My metrics export showed a counter I’d added during the same run. I looked for the inference duration histogram and came up empty. The counter told me the metrics exporter worked.I filed AI SDK issue #24229 with the reproduction. The histogram shipped in  through PR #24354. That gave me a way to check what gen_ai.client.inference.duration actually measures.I ran 25 calls on each version, five each for three generation delays, a stream and an error. A local mock model supplied the delays with timers, and I flushed the in-memory export after every call. The older version recorded zero histogram observations. The new version recorded 25, including all five errors.A span attribute does not create a metricI started with  and . The reproduction printed this:metric names: ["repro.control"]
duration attribute present: true
An empty export could have sent me back to the exporter setup. Seeing  gave me a reason to look at the integration first.The duration I saw was gen_ai.client.operation.duration, an attribute on the span. To query the distribution I needed the integration to emit a histogram.The span and the histogram take separate paths. A  sends spans to a span exporter. The metric needs a , a reader and a metric exporter.In my reproduction I registered  before setting the global meter provider. That order works with the shipped code because it looks up the meter when it records the duration.Keep the experiment localI pinned  and  at 7.0.157 and 1.0.157 for the first run, then 7.0.160 and 1.0.160 for the second configuration. Both used OpenTelemetry API 1.9.1, metrics SDK 2.8.0, trace SDK 2.7.1 and Node 24.22.2.Both configurations ran on the same Linux server. Within each one I called  sequentially and flushed after each attempt. I disabled retries and left CPU and network settings alone.Work inside the mock modelWait 40 ms, emit text, wait 160 ms, finishWait 80 ms, throw an errorAn 80 ms timer can run a little late. I wanted to see the groups separate in the export, so I used delays far enough apart to see that.Give the exporter a control measurementThe in-memory exporter let me read the measurements directly. Here’s how I set it up after registering tracing and the integration.
import { metrics } from '@opentelemetry/api';
import {
  AggregationTemporality,
  InMemoryMetricExporter,
  MeterProvider,
  PeriodicExportingMetricReader,
} from '@opentelemetry/sdk-metrics';

const exporter = new InMemoryMetricExporter(
  AggregationTemporality.CUMULATIVE,
);
const provider = new MeterProvider({
  readers: [new PeriodicExportingMetricReader({
    exporter,
    exportIntervalMillis: 60_000,
  })],
});
metrics.setGlobalMeterProvider(provider);
const control = metrics.getMeter('control')
  .createCounter('repro.control');
I incremented  and flushed after every attempt. The issue reproduction has the tracing setup and mock model. To repeat the comparison, pin both AI SDK packages together.Both counters ended at 25. Version 1.0.157 exported no inference histogram. Version 1.0.160 exported a histogram with 25 observations.Check the recorded durationsAfter each call, I subtracted the previous cumulative sum for the same attribute set, checking that its count had increased by one. That gave me a duration in seconds for that call. I converted the medians to milliseconds for the table.Median recorded inference durationThe three generation groups landed close to their timer delays. The stream and the errors contributed their own measurements.I had the mock wait 40 ms, emit text, then wait another 160 ms before closing. The caller consumed all of .First text reached my caller at a median of 43.6 ms across five calls. The histogram recorded a median of 202.3 ms for the full invocation. Those clocks start in different places, so I’d track time to first text as its own measurement.Include the calls that failThe failing mock waited 80 ms and threw Error('controlled failure'). All five attempts were recorded with retries disabled.I want to see those failures when I’m checking latency. The PR and its tests also cover cancellation and failed stream attempts during retries. My test exercised a thrown error.The count is model attempts. One user action can cause several model calls, and retries can add more attempts. Local tool execution time is outside this duration.Read the attributes beside the valueThe error point had the operation and requested model alongside . The provider was normalized from  to . The point can also carry a response model when the integration has one.I chose the model IDs as labels for the mock groups. The exported point had no prompt or response text.The updated integration docs show that telemetry.isEnabled: false turns off metrics for the call. The payload controls  and  leave duration recording on. This histogram comes from the new integration.Read cumulative counts onceI flushed after each attempt and chose cumulative aggregation. By the fifth flush, the snapshot for that group already included the previous four calls.The export puts bucket counts into discrete intervals. I’d check how my backend represents them before writing a percentile query. The integration suggests boundaries in seconds, and the metrics SDK can override them.I’d start the same way on a real service. Check the control counter and one successful call, then read the metric name, unit, attributes, count and sum in the export. Try one failed call and let one stream close. I would have missed that last 160 ms if I’d stopped reading at the first chunk.

I have been running production PostgreSQL systems for over sixteen years, and if there is one lesson that never gets old, it is this: a table that behaves beautifully at ten million rows can turn into a nightmare at five hundred million. Indexes stop helping as much as they used to, vacuum runs longer, and every maintenance window gets scarier. When I see queries slowing down despite solid indexing, partitioning is usually the next tool I reach for. Let me walk you through how I actually implement it.What Partitioning Really IsPartitioning takes one logical table and splits it into several physical sub-tables called partitions. From the application side, nothing changes. You still insert into and query one table name. Behind the scenes, PostgreSQL decides which partition each row belongs to and routes reads and writes there automatically.I like to compare it to a filing cabinet with labeled drawers. If everything is dumped into one drawer, finding last year's invoices means digging through everything ever filed. Label the drawers by year, and you go straight to "2024" and search only there. That is the entire value proposition, in one image. PostgreSQL can skip whole partitions entirely through partition pruning, so it only scans the data that could possibly match. Running VACUUM, ANALYZE, or REINDEX on a smaller partition is a lot less painful than running it on one giant table.Simple data lifecycle management. Getting rid of old data becomes DROP TABLE partition_name, which is nearly instant. Compare that to a  that has to touch millions of rows and generates a mountain of dead tuples. I can put hot, frequently accessed partitions on fast storage and let cold partitions sit on cheaper disks.The Three Strategies I Choose BetweenPostgreSQL, from version 10 onward, gives me three built-in partitioning strategies. I pick based on the shape of the data. splits rows by a range of values, most often dates or numeric IDs. This is my default choice for time-series data, things like orders, logs, or events that accumulate by day or month. splits rows by a discrete set of values, like country codes or status flags. I reach for this when the data naturally falls into named buckets rather than a continuum. spreads rows evenly across a fixed number of partitions by hashing the partition key. I use this when there is no natural range or list to key off of, but I still want the data spread out for performance and manageability.Setting Up Range PartitioningHere is how I would partition an  table that is bringing in millions of rows a year. I will partition it by , monthly.Step 1: Create the Partitioned Parent TableCREATE TABLE orders (
    id          BIGSERIAL,
    customer_id BIGINT        NOT NULL,
    amount      NUMERIC(10,2) NOT NULL,
    status      TEXT          NOT NULL,
    created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ   NOT NULL
) PARTITION BY RANGE (created_at);
Something worth keeping in mind here: the parent table never actually stores any rows. It is a purely logical container that PostgreSQL uses to route data to the right child partition.Step 2: Create the PartitionsCREATE TABLE orders_2024_01
    PARTITION OF orders
    FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-01-01') TO ('2024-02-01');

CREATE TABLE orders_2024_02
    PARTITION OF orders
    FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-02-01') TO ('2024-03-01');

CREATE TABLE orders_2024_03
    PARTITION OF orders
    FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-03-01') TO ('2024-04-01');
One detail I always double check with new team members: the range bounds are inclusive at the bottom and exclusive at the top. A row dated exactly  lands in , not .I create indexes on the parent table, and as of PostgreSQL 11 they propagate down to every partition automatically. That saves me from having to remember to index each child individually.CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_id ON orders (customer_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_created_at  ON orders (created_at);
Step 4: Insert Data as NormalINSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount, status, created_at)
VALUES (42, 199.99, 'completed', '2024-01-15 10:30:00+00');
I do not have to tell PostgreSQL which partition this belongs in. It reads the  value and routes it to on its own.Step 5: Confirm Partition Pruning Is Actually HappeningThis step is not optional in my process. I always verify pruning with  before I trust a partitioning setup in production.EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders
WHERE created_at >= '2024-02-01'
  AND created_at <  '2024-03-01';
If the setup is correct, the plan should only touch . If I see the planner scanning every partition, something in my query or my partition key is off, and I go fix it before moving on.When the Key Is Categorical: List PartitioningIf I am partitioning something like a  table by region rather than by time, list partitioning is the right tool.CREATE TABLE customers (
    id     BIGSERIAL,
    name   TEXT NOT NULL,
    region TEXT NOT NULL
) PARTITION BY LIST (region);

CREATE TABLE customers_us
    PARTITION OF customers
    FOR VALUES IN ('US', 'CA');

CREATE TABLE customers_eu
    PARTITION OF customers
    FOR VALUES IN ('DE', 'FR', 'GB', 'NL');

CREATE TABLE customers_apac
    PARTITION OF customers
    FOR VALUES IN ('AU', 'JP', 'SG', 'IN');
Each region code is explicitly assigned to a bucket, which makes this strategy easy to reason about and easy to explain to the rest of the team.When There Is No Natural Key: Hash PartitioningSometimes there is genuinely no range or category to split on, and I just need the data spread evenly for performance and manageability. That is when I use hash partitioning. Here I am splitting an  table into four partitions by .CREATE TABLE events (
    id         BIGSERIAL,
    user_id    BIGINT NOT NULL,
    event_type TEXT   NOT NULL,
    payload    JSONB,
    occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL
) PARTITION BY HASH (user_id);

CREATE TABLE events_p0 PARTITION OF events FOR VALUES WITH (MODULUS 4, REMAINDER 0);
CREATE TABLE events_p1 PARTITION OF events FOR VALUES WITH (MODULUS 4, REMAINDER 1);
CREATE TABLE events_p2 PARTITION OF events FOR VALUES WITH (MODULUS 4, REMAINDER 2);
CREATE TABLE events_p3 PARTITION OF events FOR VALUES WITH (MODULUS 4, REMAINDER 3);
Catching the Rows You Forgot About: Default PartitionsEspecially during development, or when I am not one hundred percent sure every possible value has a home, I set up a default partition to catch anything that does not match an existing rule.CREATE TABLE orders_default
    PARTITION OF orders DEFAULT;
This has saved me more than once from a failed insert at 2 a.m. because someone introduced a new status value nobody told me about.Automating Partition CreationI never want to be the person manually creating next month's partition by hand, and neither should you. In production, I write a function like this and schedule it.CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION create_monthly_partition(target_date DATE)
RETURNS VOID AS $$
DECLARE
    partition_name TEXT;
    start_date     DATE;
    end_date       DATE;
BEGIN
    start_date     := DATE_TRUNC('month', target_date);
    end_date       := start_date + INTERVAL '1 month';
    partition_name := 'orders_' || TO_CHAR(start_date, 'YYYY_MM');

    EXECUTE FORMAT(
        'CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS %I PARTITION OF orders FOR VALUES FROM (%L) TO (%L)',
        partition_name, start_date, end_date
    );
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

-- Create partitions for the next 3 months
SELECT create_monthly_partition(DATE_TRUNC('month', NOW()) + (n || ' month')::INTERVAL)
FROM generate_series(0, 2) AS n;
I schedule this with  when it is available, or with an external scheduler like a cron job or Airflow when it is not. Either way, I make sure I always have a few months of partitions created ahead of time, so an inserting application never hits a missing partition.This is where partitioning earns its keep for data lifecycle management. Instead of running a slow, lock-heavy  that chews through WAL and leaves bloat behind, I just drop the partition.-- Instantly drop a year's worth of data
DROP TABLE orders_2022_01;
DROP TABLE orders_2022_02;
-- ... etc
If I need to keep the data around for archival purposes rather than destroying it, I detach it instead:ALTER TABLE orders DETACH PARTITION orders_2022_01;
-- Partition now exists as a standalone table, archive or export it
A few things trip people up every single time, so let me save you the pain.Your primary key has to include the partition key. PostgreSQL cannot enforce uniqueness across partitions unless the partition key is part of the key.-- This will fail:
ALTER TABLE orders ADD PRIMARY KEY (id);

-- This works:
ALTER TABLE orders ADD PRIMARY KEY (id, created_at);
Foreign keys referencing a partitioned table are not supported. Foreign keys going out from a partitioned table are fine, but nothing else can point at it with a foreign key constraint.Partition pruning only works if the partition key shows up in your WHERE clause. Leave it out, and PostgreSQL has no choice but to scan every partition.Do not go overboard with granularity. Hundreds of tiny partitions will hurt planning time more than they help. For time-series data, I have found monthly or quarterly partitions to be the sweet spot in most systems I have run.Checking Your Partition SetupWhen I want a quick sanity check on what partitions exist and how big they have gotten, I run this:-- List all partitions of a table
SELECT inhrelid::regclass AS partition_name,
       pg_get_expr(c.relpartbound, inhrelid) AS partition_bound,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(inhrelid)) AS size
FROM   pg_inherits
JOIN   pg_class c ON c.oid = inhrelid
WHERE  inhparent = 'orders'::regclass
ORDER  BY partition_name;
When I Choose Not to PartitionPartitioning is not free. It adds real operational complexity, and I skip it when any of the following apply:The table is under roughly ten million rows. Indexing alone usually handles that fine.There is no natural partition key to work with.Most of the queries against the table are aggregations across the whole dataset anyway, in which case partitioning will not move the needle much.The team maintaining the system is not ready to take on the added operational complexity.PostgreSQL's declarative partitioning is mature and, honestly, not that hard to implement once you understand the shape of your data. Here is what I keep coming back to:Use range partitioning for time-series and sequential data.Use list partitioning for categorical or enum-style columns.Use hash partitioning when you need even distribution and have no natural key.Always include the partition key in your primary key.Automate partition creation, and drop old partitions instead of running expensive deletes.Always verify partition pruning with  so you know your queries are actually benefiting.My advice after years of doing this: pick one table that is currently causing you pain, instrument it properly, implement partitioning, and measure the before and after. Once you see the difference for yourself, you will not need convincing on the next one.

I used to manage my products through dashboards. Check analytics here, schedule posts there, pull metrics from another tab. Constant context-switching.Now, I do all of it by talking to Claude.Not through some hacked-together integration: through MCP servers I built for my own products. Model Context Protocol is the standard Anthropic published for giving AI assistants structured access to external tools. I built servers for both Spellcast and Lunary. Here's how, and why it changed everything.What MCP actually is (in one paragraph).MCP is a protocol that lets you define a set of "tools", which are functions with typed inputs and outputs that Claude can call during a conversation. Instead of copy-pasting data into a chat window, Claude calls your API directly and works with the live result.The architecture: your MCP server exposes tools over stdio (or HTTP). Claude Code discovers them and can call them when relevant. You write the server once; it works in any MCP-compatible client.Spellcast is my social scheduling platform. The MCP server exposes the full API as typed tools:server.addTool({
  name: 'list_posts',
  description: 'List scheduled, published, draft, or failed posts with optional filters',
  parameters: z.object({
    account_set_id: z.string().optional(),
    status: z.enum(['draft', 'scheduled', 'published', 'failed']).optional(),
    limit: z.number().default(50),
  }),
  execute: async ({ account_set_id, status, limit }) => {
    const posts = await db.query.posts.findMany({
      where: buildWhereClause({ account_set_id, status }),
      limit,
    });
    return posts;
  },
});
What I can do from a Claude conversation:: see everything in the queue: schedule new content: edit scheduled posts: push articles to Dev.to and Hashnode: pull engagement data / : manage AI personas: create post variations using my brand voice: manage posting personasThe practical workflow: I describe what I want, and Claude calls the tools. "Optimize the hooks on my scheduled tweets" becomes Claude reading every post, identifying weak openers, and rewriting them in batches.Lunary is my astrology platform. The MCP exposes product analytics and content tools:server.addTool({
  name: 'get_dashboard',
  description: 'Get key product metrics: MAU, MRR, retention, feature usage',
  parameters: z.object({}),
  execute: async () => {
    const metrics = await getProductMetrics();
    return metrics;
  },
});
: MAU, MRR, retention at a glance: engagement breakdown: which parts of the app people actually use: how well I'm holding users over time: search the 2,000+ article content library: patterns Claude surfaces from the data: identify at-risk usersWhat the workflow actually looks like "Check Lunary metrics and tell me what's changed since last week."Claude calls , , . Returns a summary. Surfaces what moved. "Schedule a thread about the SEO growth for the sammii account."Claude calls  to find the right persona,  to load my voice profile,  to write five thread variations, presents them, waits for my pick, then calls  to schedule it. "Those tweets from February with weak hooks, fix them."Claude calls  for the date range, reads each one, identifies weak openers, writes improvements, calls  in batches.No dashboards. No tab-switching. Just conversation with full product access.npm install @modelcontextprotocol/sdk zod
import { McpServer } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js';
import { StdioServerTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js';
import { z } from 'zod';

const server = new McpServer({ name: 'my-product', version: '1.0.0' });

server.tool(
  'get_stats',
  'Get product statistics',
  {},
  async () => {
    const stats = await fetchMyProductStats();
    return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: JSON.stringify(stats) }] };
  }
);

const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
await server.connect(transport);
Wire it into Claude Code's settings:{
  "mcpServers": {
    "my-product": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["/path/to/my-mcp/dist/index.js"]
    }
  }
}
Your product's API is now accessible from any Claude conversation.Why it matters for solo founders.The power isn't just convenience. Claude can operate across your entire product context in a single conversation: read your analytics, read your content queue, read your brand voice, and produce work that's coherent across all of them without you manually bridging the gap.I estimate MCP saves me 5-8 hours a week in dashboard time and manual data-pulling. More importantly, it removes the friction that stops small tasks from getting done. If checking metrics requires three tabs and a copy-paste, you do it less. If it's one sentence, you do it constantly and catch things early.
